  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1007 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nLarge scale subsidence behind a departing a digging trough will\ncontinue to promote cooler than normal temperatures early\nWednesday morning and Wednesday afternoon. Temperatures in the\nlow to mid 20s will be widespread across the region to start\nearly Wednesday morning. Temperatures will struggle to climb out\nof the 50s, with perhaps the exception being the far western\nzones, Wednesday afternoon. Temperatures  Altogether, these \ntemperatures are about 3-9F below normal. This pattern will also\nfavor continued dry conditions, at least until this weekend.\n\nBy Friday, the pattern flips to a more mild airmass. 850mb\ntemperature anomalies are expected to be +6-10C both Friday and\nSaturday ahead of the next wave. Regarding the next wave and\nprecipitation chance for the area, global models depict varying\nsolutions for the late Saturday night and early Sunday morning\ntimeframe. The 18z GFS appears to be the outlier solution with \nnot much of a wave to speak of. However, the latest ENS, ICON, \nand GDPS show more of a rather small, but strong and compact \nvorticity maxima tracking into the northwestern U.S. and then \ntraversing the central and northern Rockies at the start of the\nweekend, resulting in possible surface cyclogenesis south of the\nforecast area. In any solution, the fast and progressive system\nwill likely have limited moisture to work with. Current NBM \nPOPs are 15-25%, which seems reasonable given this setup. It \nwill be close with the type of precipitation. NBM favors just \nlight rain showers, but with colder air quickly moving in, some \nsnowflakes may not be ruled out. If snow were to become more \nprevalent, minimal impacts are still expected given speed of the\nsystem, marginal surface temperatures, and light precipitation \nrates.",
